    "result_text": "All tests green. Here's a summary of what I changed.\n\n## What the spec required\n\nMake `householdId` a **deterministic** function of the household key `(normalizedLastName, postcode)` \u2014 the first 12 hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode` \u2014 so same-household owners share it automatically, and rewire duplicate detection, `sharesHousehold`, and the household-size computation around that.\n\n## Changes\n\n**`AssignHousehold.java`** \u2014 rewritten. It no longer scans the repository or back-fills existing owners, and no longer depends on `sharesHousehold`. It now sets `owner.householdId` to the deterministic hash of `(normalizedLastName, postcode)` for every owner (null when there's no postcode, since without a postcode there's no household). Because it's derived purely from the key, all members of a household get the identical value regardless of creation order.\n\n**`EnsureUniqueIdentity.java`** \u2014 duplicate detection now keys off the computed `householdId`. It rejects with 409 when:\n- an existing owner has the **same `householdId`** (same last name + postcode = same household) \u2014 **unless the request sets `sharesHousehold`**, which bypasses this block so the owner is created as a declared member; or\n- the WHOLE `identityKey` matches an existing owner (a truly identical record is always a conflict).\n\n**`FlagPossibleDuplicate.java`** \u2014 a declared household member (`sharesHousehold=true`) is now never flagged as a suspected duplicate (early return before the soft-match scan). \"A declared member is not a suspected duplicate.\"\n\n**`AssignHouseholdSize.java`** \u2014 unchanged logic (already counts by `householdId`); it now benefits from the deterministic id automatically. Javadoc updated to drop the old \"back-fill\" wording.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `householdId`, `sharesHousehold`, and `identityKey` descriptions/examples to match the new deterministic, postcode-keyed behavior.\n\n## Verification\n\n`./mvnw -o test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The new `AcceptanceTest.coreSameLastNameAndPostcodeShareHouseholdId` passes: same last name + postcode yield the same `householdId` (the second owner using `sharesHousehold` to bypass the duplicate block), and a different last name yields a different one.",
